content:
 I came across an old scrap from my writing class at UoP. It&rsquo;s a perfect scene for The Price of Dreams, but alas, I didn&rsquo;t find it sooner. It really catches the mood I was in when I had the dream. I prefer not to make any revisions in the middle of a draft&ndash;especially when I&rsquo;m posting the work in progress, so I&rsquo;m setting it out on the side until I&rsquo;m done. If I still think it works on the second draft, that&rsquo;s when I&rsquo;ll work it in. [Technically, I haven&rsquo;t gotten to the second draft, but it works nice as a prologue, so I&rsquo;m adding it in here.]
Original Post Date: Jan 20, 2014, 2:14:31 PM – A Short Scene Writing Assignmant &ldquo;Unravelled&rdquo;
Classic rock played softly in the background as my tired mind twisted and turned around the notion of sleep. I longed for it. I knew I had too little time for it. It was taunting, cruel; only coming when so many other things needed doing. Sleep danced beyond my grasp. In its wake shifted the ashes of so many thoughts, dreams, hopes and frustrations. I wondered how I came to this. I tried to wrap my mind around something worthy of the space my words were channeled toward. A class. An obligation, slipping, drifting through my hands like fine, dry sand.
My eyes burned, dry tears cracking as I blinked and tried to focus. When was it simple? When was it easy to break things down into entities and attributes? It was back when those words had different meanings for me and spoke of living things, not atoms of fact. Fact. Facts. The cold dry ache of inches upon inches of snow that drank every drop of moisture from the air, left my lips and throat parched. I lay atop my blanket, too tired to chase down inspiration, rising again and again to chase away my thirst. Useless. In between sips, the dry, bitter taste of smoke curled upon my tongue.
With nothing else to write, I wrote down my insomniac musings. I imagined someone reading it and finding it depressing, but I was indifferent. The wasteland my mind wandered in was so far beyond depression. I was tempted to sigh, but I stopped at a deep breath and closed my eyes. There was patience deeper than my frustration. There was dedication in spite of this loss of coherent thought and purpose. Not this night. This night was not a good night. I was too drunk on confusion. I&rsquo;d had a thought, an urge to participate, but thinking was more like dreaming at the moment.
So I told myself, &ldquo;I should sleep.&rdquo; A moment stretched on while that thought sank in, then I groaned, &ldquo;I really need to sleep.&rdquo;

 Jan 11, 2014, 7:29:20 PM
I woke up disoriented, in a strange place, feeling absolutely exhausted. I didn&rsquo;t know what woke me or how long I&rsquo;d been asleep, but it seemed like I&rsquo;d been trying to get a decent night&rsquo;s sleep for years. When my life started falling apart, and the specter of homelessness started to loom over me, I never imagined the thing I would miss most would be a good night&rsquo;s sleep.
Not that I ever had an easy time sleeping before. I mean, part of what brought me down was difficulty sleeping. Some of that came from my creative side. When inspiration hit, whether it was a story I was trying to write or a picture I wanted to paint, the creative impulse took hold and didn&rsquo;t let go until I passed out from exhaustion. While I was caught up in it, nothing else mattered. Those were the only moments I ever really felt certain of anything.
Unfortunately, I was not making a living from my art and writing. I had to take whatever odd jobs I could get to pay the bills, and the income was rarely enough to do more. If I could not get a full-time position, I got two or three part time jobs, or I cycled through temp and contract work. At the end of the day, with time taken out for meals, chores, errands, bathing and sleep, there wasn&rsquo;t much left for writing or drawing.
So, occasionally, I sacrificed sleep for art.
More often, I just lay awake battling my personal demons. The truth is, I did not write or draw anywhere near enough to stay good at it. Worse, the demons started to creep into my art and I found myself creating things I didn&rsquo;t want to share with anyone. Okay, I wasn&rsquo;t dreaming up Fight Club, but my art became my therapy and the results were way too personal to hang on a wall or publish.
I pushed those thoughts back and shifted my weight, trying to find a comfortable position and discovering that my bed for the night was a couch. It had the generic familiarity all couches get when you&rsquo;ve spent a while couch surfing. The space around me was unfamiliar, and I could not get my eyes to focus on anything in the dark. I stopped counting after four walls, concluding that the room was open to a few others.
That seemed like more shelter than I was accustomed to, so I pulled my coat in tighter around myself and closed my eyes. I missed the warmth and weight of a good blanket, but the absence of night&rsquo;s bitter, biting cold was a relief. I had a pretty decent chance of getting back to sleep. My thoughts were starting to disconnect and spin themselves into images when I heard it again, a muted conversation.
For a few moments, the words crashed over me in a formless wash of surf. One voice had a deep, rumbling undercurrent, while the other sang like wind and children dancing in the sunlight across the sand under the distant cry of birds. I couldn&rsquo;t make out the conversation, but the second voice stirred a memory&ndash;unusually vivid as memories can be on the verge of unconsciousness.
I&rsquo;d been walking, lost in thought and oblivious to the fading light when I&rsquo;d first heard it. I&rsquo;d glanced up and came to an absolute stop at the sight of her. I&rsquo;d never seen her before, but I knew every detail by heart. I had spent years in vain, trying to draw her, to paint her, and I could not believe my eyes. I stared, waiting to spot the fatal flaw, expecting the illusion to collapse as I focused on her.
She finished talking to the person she had bumped into and resumed walking, headed away from the store she had just come out of.
I blinked and rubbed my eyes, but my vision did not change. This girl, whoever she was, was real. And, she was walking away. I fell into her wake without even thinking about it. I recognized the surge of energy that kicked in, the sudden pounding of my pulse, and remembered other times when I had chased after a glimpse, full of hope and bound for disappointment. I always looked twice at girls with red hair, but it was never the right red hair, the right face, the right form. I was never sure what I was looking for, only that I would recognize it when I saw it.
Now that I&rsquo;d seen it, I felt torn between awe and agony.
It wasn&rsquo;t really necessary to think it through, as I followed. The first time I&rsquo;d felt this attraction, I thought it was love at first sight. I stumbled into the predictable fiasco, making an absolute idiot of myself and embarrassing the hell out of the poor girl&ndash;or completely freaking her out. Probably both.
I learned something from it, though. I didn&rsquo;t find that first girl any more attractive than other girls I&rsquo;d seen. I was just caught off guard by her resemblance to someone who was intensely and inexplicably imprinted on my brain. I never noticed it before, but I had always measured people against that image, like an archetype or an ideal.
I looked for her the way a person looks for a lost loved one.
It sort of made sense. I was 5 years old when I was adopted, and I lost touch with everyone I knew in the process. I had no idea who this girl was, but she clearly represented someone very important to me, someone torn away from me who I remained connected to at an unconscious level.
It&rsquo;s as good a theory as any. All I really knew, was that I had this girl on the brain. Jung would call her my Anima. I tended to think of her as my Muse. A very frustrating muse I was never able to see clearly enough to draw or paint, or even capture in writing. She was kind of like the sun, shedding light and warmth on everything around her, but I couldn&rsquo;t look directly at her without going blind.
Not the version of her that lived in my head, anyway.
I knew the girl I was following could not possibly her, but she was a perfect match for the template in my head. She was about five inches shorter than me, but tall for a girl. Slender, but obviously athletic&ndash;she was more of a dancer than a gymnast, and probably a swimmer. Her movements had a quiet grace, a confident poise. There was something slightly feline in her features. A heart shaped face, straight nose, generous mouth and eyes like a storm on the ocean. Her dark red hair was full and long, with rogue curls rippling free from flowing waves.
A lot to take in at a glance, and a glance could never be enough&ndash;I was not the only one on that street inclined to stare at her.
Apart from wanting to get a better look at her, I had no idea what to do if I caught up to her. I was living rough and looked like it. I&rsquo;d scare the crap out of her if I tried to approach her, and even if I didn&rsquo;t, what could I say&ndash;&ldquo;I need to look at you&rdquo;? Creepy enough, but the truth was, I wanted to study her for hours. Draw her. Paint her. Capture her image so I could never forget it. I was an artist, but if I said so, she&rsquo;d probably think it was a pick-up line.
I was pretty sure I had a sketch book and a few pencils in my book bag, so maybe if she stopped somewhere for a while, I could perch somewhere nearby and do some random sketching. Provide a little evidence of my trade before trying to make contact.
Wow. That sounded stalker-creepy just thinking it to myself. I know it comes from being a writer. Planning scenes out comes as naturally to me as breathing. The problem is, that kind of scenario works in a story because the characters are generally innocent of intent&ndash;or they&rsquo;re bad guys.
I don&rsquo;t think about my appearance much, but I could see my reflection in every window we passed. Tall, dark and not particularly handsome. Dressed in black; worn leather shoes, denim jeans, t-shirt and leather jacket, with a black bag at my hip. Oval face, worn features&ndash;pretty sinister looking in the early evening light&ndash;with long, fine dark hair pulled back into a thin pony-tail.
Some girls find me attractive, but if I was a girl, I think I&rsquo;d find me kind of frightening.
While I was fretting about that, I realized I was catching up with her. She had stopped several times to talk to people, and before I could even wonder what she was up to, one of the people in the group she was talking to tapped her on the shoulder and pointed directly at me.
I resisted the urge to look behind me as she turned in my direction. She gave me a quick look, down to my shoes and up again to meet my eyes&ndash;which had to be wide with disbelief. I stopped in my tracks and stared back, as she took inventory of my appearance. Any doubts I had about her appearance fled, along with my wits, as she seemed to run through a mental checklist.
I didn&rsquo;t know how I knew. The body language was as subtle as it gets, but I knew the process from the inside out. She&rsquo;d never seen me before, but she knew exactly what she was looking for. Part of me thought that made perfect sense, but the rational part was trying to seize up in denial. While I stood, brainlocked, she nodded and excused herself from the group of strangers she&rsquo;d been talking to.
She shook her head as she approached me. Coming to a stop, she gave me a final appraisal and announced casually, &ldquo;You look like shit. That threw me for a moment.&rdquo;
The gears in my head slipped and words spilled out. &ldquo;This is where I&rsquo;m supposed to wake up.&rdquo;
Her eyes locked on mine, and she smiled. &ldquo;You look like you&rsquo;re about to pass out, actually.&rdquo;
To my complete mortification, that&rsquo;s exactly what I did.
I blinked my eyes and rubbed my face, dread certainty settling in as I came fully awake. I&rsquo;d had dreams like this before. Sometimes it was just a glimpse in the kaleidoscope of fantasy and memory that stews in my sleep. Sometimes it seemed as real to me as anything I&rsquo;d ever known. Sometimes more real. The hard part was always waking up and having it all slip away.
Before that rationalization could seat me firmly back in line with harsh reality, a voice intruded. Her voice. &ldquo;Have you finally gotten around to waking up?&rdquo; she asked, stepping out of the shadows of an adjoining room and into the fall of light from a window somewhere above me&ndash;in the wall to the right of the couch, actually.
&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know,&rdquo; I confessed, certain I should feel even more disoriented, but mostly clinging to the hope that returning consciousness usually crushed. &ldquo;I could just be dreaming I&rsquo;m awake. I should be asking where I am, but all that really matters is that you&rsquo;re here. If I am dreaming, I hope I don&rsquo;t wake up.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Works for me,&rdquo; she replied, &ldquo;as long as you don&rsquo;t pass out again.&rdquo;
I nodded, then shook my head. &ldquo;I can&rsquo;t believe I did that. I don&rsquo;t think that&rsquo;s ever happened to me before.&rdquo; I slipped my legs off the couch and sat up. The closest experience I could think of was nodding off after a big meal&ndash;or a few days without sleep. In those cases, I&rsquo;d feel my head going into a fog and could try to fight off sleep. Before I blacked out, I felt like my brain was in overdrive, seeing the world with unnatural clarity and then: snap; out go the lights.
&ldquo;Sorry about that. I didn&rsquo;t plan to catch you off guard like that. You were harder to find than I expected,&rdquo; she explained, sliding gracefully into a chair across from me.
I couldn&rsquo;t help smiling. &ldquo;I never expected to find you at all.&rdquo; I took a deep breath and sighed. &ldquo;You&rsquo;re not surprised by that,&rdquo; I observed, the intuitive part of me screaming about the implications my rational mind still refused to consider. &ldquo;I still don&rsquo;t know why I was looking, and it never really occurred to me you&rsquo;d come searching for me.&rdquo;
She shrugged. &ldquo;I&rsquo;m just sorry it took so long. You&rsquo;re older than I was expecting. You weren&rsquo;t supposed to be homeless.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I feel the same, if it makes any difference,&rdquo; I tried to joke. I got the ghost of a smile in reply, but then she feel silent, waiting. I could have asked why she was looking for me, but I was afraid I already knew. I&rsquo;d spent a long time thinking about the impossible, looking at it from the outside. It should have felt more confusing to look at it from the inside, and in many ways it was. Questions were flooding through me, but I couldn&rsquo;t bring myself to ask any of them, because I felt like I already knew all the answers.
It was scaring the crap out of me.
She looked calm. Completely confident in herself, her expression touched with sympathy and wry amusement. She knew I was on the verge of freaking out. &ldquo;I&rsquo;d introduce myself, but you know me better than I do, so the real question&ndash;the one that you&rsquo;re trying like crazy not to ask&ndash;is, what am I doing here?&rdquo;
I opened my mouth, but nothing would come out.
She laughed, and then she sighed. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s okay. I freaked out pretty bad when was reading the books for the first time.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Books?&rdquo; It was not the most articulate question I ever asked, but it squeaked out past the collision of other questions that leapt to mind.
&ldquo;Your books,&rdquo; she clarified with a piercing glare. &ldquo;The books about me. The books you don&rsquo;t seem to have written yet. That&rsquo;s why I was looking for you.&rdquo;
Just like that, all the questions went silent. I didn&rsquo;t need them. I could not deny that, impossible as it seemed, I probably did have all the answers. I knew it, because I knew the exact scene she was talking about. I&rsquo;d thought about it for years, trying to figure out how to write it.
It was a simple premise, and one that seemed brilliant every time I looked at it. In the fantasy story I wrestled with endlessly on my word processor, my protagonist, an embodiment of my muse, stumbled across a collection of books from another world. Books that focused on her and her adventures. It was kind of my own take on Heinlein&rsquo;s World-As-Myth paradigm.
If I&rsquo;d been able to write my stories, and managed to get them published, I would have thought someone was trying to punk me. As she pointed out, however, I didn&rsquo;t write them. How could someone punk me with something that only existed in my head?
I stood up, skirted the coffee table between us, and knelt beside her chair. She watched as I put a hand on her knee. Under a layer of synthetic-fiber material that, to the best of my knowledge, existed only in fiction, her knee felt warm and solid.
&ldquo;I&rsquo;ve never been prone to vivid hallucinations. Some drugs really mess with my senses, but my mind has never played a trick like this on me,&rdquo; I commented, trying to wrap my head around what was happening.
She gave a harsh laugh, &ldquo;This is just a taste of what I went through, reading those books.&rdquo;
I nodded. The books may not have gotten finished, or published, but I spent decades trying to write them&ndash;thinking every scene through over and over, trying to get them right. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve got an idea. I never got that part of the story perfect, so I can only guess what you really went through. Some versions were pretty ugly,&rdquo; I admitted.
She stared at me for a moment. Something passed behind her eyes, washing out a subtle tension I had not noticed until it was gone. &ldquo;I think I finally get it. Part of it, at least,&rdquo; she said, to herself, I assumed.
&ldquo;If any of this makes sense to you, you&rsquo;re way ahead of me,&rdquo; I told her. I returned to the couch, and kicked my feet up on the coffee table.
&ldquo;There&rsquo;s still some paradox involved, but not the kind I was most afraid of,&rdquo; she said, pulling her feet up under herself, as she shifted into a more comfortable position on the chair. &ldquo;For a while, I was afraid that finding you would be like meeting my maker. I&rsquo;m sorry, but, well&rdquo; she made a broad gesture in clear reference to my appearance, &ldquo;you&rsquo;re not what I picture when I think of the Goddess.&rdquo;
I looked down at myself and had to smirk. &ldquo;You would not be alone in that!&rdquo;
She screwed up her nose and gave me a lopsided smile, and we both burst out laughing. When I got my breath back, I said, &ldquo;I can almost imagine how this is possible, you being here, me being able to write about you, if I slip into writer mode. I don&rsquo;t know if I could explain it, and it&rsquo;s just not possible at all in the world I live in. But it&rsquo;s happening. You&rsquo;re here. How?&rdquo;
She sighed heavily, &ldquo;Damn. I was hoping you&rsquo;d be able to tell me. But, if you haven&rsquo;t finished the story, then I guess you don&rsquo;t know as much as I expected. I was hoping you were the key to getting back home. Now, I&rsquo;m thinking this is how the paradox gets resolved.&rdquo;
I was pretty sure I could follow that, but it couldn&rsquo;t hurt to be sure. &ldquo;You think you&rsquo;re here because I didn&rsquo;t write the books?&rdquo;
She cocked her head to the side in thought, gnawing on her lower lip for a moment, before shrugging and giving me a nod. &ldquo;You seem to already be tuned in to me somehow, but there&rsquo;s some kind of interference. You said there are different versions of what happens to me. The story I read was accurate, but the story you&rsquo;re writing isn&rsquo;t finished. Now that I am here, I can help you finish the story. Maybe, in the process, we can figure out how I get home.&rdquo;
I took a moment to digest that. I had to. I&rsquo;ve got a pretty open mind, and honestly, I wished for most of my life that the people and places in the best sci-fi and fantasy novels were real. I&rsquo;m sure I&rsquo;m not alone in feeling that way. Nothing in my experience&ndash;short of dreams&ndash;prepared me to confront the possibility that they were. In dreams, you can take the impossible in stride. Stop to confront it, try to grasp it rationally, and you just wake yourself up.
So, I tried to take it slow. &ldquo;You seem real. God knows, I want you to be real. So, it follows that your home is real; all those things I struggled to write about are real&ndash;gods, angels, demons, dragons, magic, and all of that. It&rsquo;s all real.&rdquo;
&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t think &lsquo;real&rsquo; is the right word,&rdquo; she said, shaking her head. &ldquo;It&rsquo;s more accurate to say those things are true. They exist independent of the world you live in, and the people in your world don&rsquo;t really know how to engage something like that. Not even your own souls. When you&rsquo;re trapped in the box, it doesn&rsquo;t matter what tools lie outside the box.&rdquo;
&ldquo;And the box is reality?&rdquo; I asked.
&ldquo;No. To put it very simply, the box is life,&rdquo; she said, holding up a hand before I could interrupt. &ldquo;The trap is thinking reality exists only inside the box; that there is nothing outside the box to engage in. The trap is when the box is your only reality; when you deny anything outside the limits of the box.&rdquo;
I considered that for a moment, before venturing, &ldquo;So, it&rsquo;s not accurate to say all the things I know of from your world are all real, but it would be right to say that those are all things that have their own reality?&rdquo;
She raised her eyebrows and gave a little nod. &ldquo;That would work.&rdquo;

 Jan 13, 2014, 4:49:19 PM
Under normal circumstances, I&rsquo;d be inclined to explore the premise and implications of actual, discrete realities, but when the embodiment of your imagination drags your unconscious ass off the street to discuss your failure to write her story, well, we&rsquo;re obviously not talking about a normal circumstance. She&rsquo;d already admitted she did not have an explanation for how she got here, so I considered the next question.
The story. Her story. If anyone should know it, it was her. If anything should be impossible, it would be me knowing her story the way I did. At least, that would normally be the case with the life story of someone you met on the street. Again, normal did not quite apply to this situation. Without a single attempt to establish the details of her story, we&rsquo;d already referred to it the way two old acquaintances addressed a shared experience.
On the other hand, there was one thing the story could not account for.
&ldquo;So, how is it that you know me?&rdquo; I asked, finally scratching at the itch that started bothering me from the moment she first spoke to me. &ldquo;I always intended to publish under a pseudonym&ndash;a pen name.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Oh, you did,&rdquo; she admitted, giving me an irritated look. &ldquo;Fortunately, the books I found about me were just a subset of the collection of books written about you.&rdquo; Her irritation evolved into amused satisfaction at my shock. Before I recovered, she went on, &ldquo;But the truth is, I was dreaming about you for a long time before I got access to that library.&rdquo;
I blinked. A smug voice in the back of my head told me I should have known, but had chosen not to. Rather than pursue that thought, I focused on her. &ldquo;You dreamed about me?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Um,&rdquo; she looked off to the side and cleared her throat. &ldquo;It was more like, I had nightmares about you.&rdquo; Her eyes turned back to me. &ldquo;Nightmares about being you.&rdquo;
My mouth went dry and my throat suddenly tried to close up. I dragged my feet off the coffee table and planted them on the floor, leaning forward to brace my elbows on my knees. I knew the story. I knew she had always been plagued by nightmares, but&hellip; &ldquo;I thought your nightmares were about the massacre&ndash;the night your&hellip; mother died.&rdquo;
She looked away again, and closed her eyes; concealing the stab of an old wound. Her reply was a bit tight, &ldquo;That&rsquo;s where they often start&ndash;and end. In the middle, they go down stranger, darker paths that seem to go on forever and I get so lost&hellip; I can barely remember who I am.&rdquo;
My gut clenched. &ldquo;And that&rsquo;s when you dream of being me?&rdquo;
She hesitated before nodding.
I pushed up and stalked away in a sudden fury of conflicting emotion. There was nowhere to go. No way I wanted to walk away from her. I didn&rsquo;t even really see anything but her, and yet I felt like I was riding a rocket out of the atmosphere. I didn&rsquo;t get more than a few steps before I stopped, and let the thunder roll through me.
This is what mountains feel like in that terrifying moment they remember they are volcanos. In the brief passion of exploding; shattering and dissolving into choking clouds of ash. When they turn inside out, revealing their hollow shells and molten cores.
&ldquo;It&rsquo;s not the same,&rdquo; I breathed, hands clenching into fists as if I could keep from flying away if I just held on to myself tight enough. &ldquo;I dream about you, sometimes. Sometimes, for an instant, I&rsquo;m someone like you. I&rsquo;ve never really dreamed of being you,&rdquo; I declared in a hollow, aching voice.
&ldquo;Not when you&rsquo;re asleep,&rdquo; she corrected softly. Her hand grasped me by the shoulder and with firm but gentle strength she turned me to face her. Met me eye to eye. &ldquo;But you dream about me almost every moment you&rsquo;re awake. I am always on your mind, and that&rsquo;s why it&rsquo;s such a nightmare to be you.&rdquo;
The emotion that thrust me to my feet was spent, and for one endless instant the whole universe stood silent and still. Then I was in free fall. Her hands slid down my arms, gently closing about my fists. With that same firm, feather-light touch, she brought our hands up into our line of sight.
I already knew this metaphor. I used it to describe what I call the point paradox. When you hold on to nothing, you grasp yourself. It&rsquo;s the fundamental existential paradox. &ldquo;A story isn&rsquo;t the words on a page. Words are just the ashes of a fire that burns only in the mind. My story, burning in your mind; you, consciously unleashing your imagination; you dream of being me when you&rsquo;re wide awake,&rdquo; she asserted passionately.
What could I say. A person could define the creative process in other terms, but her description was weirdly accurate and essentially true. The tricky part, as always in the case of a truth, was knowing what it actually meant. I pulled my hands free and ran my palms over my face, covering my eyes. The mask I wear is one I cannot take off, but she managed to strip it away almost effortlessly, shattering the lie that lets me function as a human being.
&ldquo;I guess that&rsquo;s the problem,&rdquo; I confessed after a moment, from behind the shelter of my hands. &ldquo;I had a dream instead of a life. It was a good dream; too good, really. For all the pain and horror, and all the paradoxes, too good to be true. A great story, but&hellip;&rdquo; I took a deep breath and sighed, dropping my hands. &ldquo;I was looking for a way to survive, and it was too strange and complicated a story to find a marketable audience.&rdquo;
She regarded me thoughtfully for a moment. &ldquo;I think I understand,&rdquo; she finally said, drifting toward an end table and turning on a lamp. As the light spilled into the room, she gestured at the lamp&rsquo;s twin on the matching table at the other end of the couch. She continued speaking as I lit the other light. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve passed through your life like a leaf in a hurricane. I saw more than was in the books, but I have to guess at the proper sequence.&rdquo;
I crossed my arms and looked down. &ldquo;If I&rsquo;d had any balance in my life, I might have been able to finish writing and publish. I don&rsquo;t know if I&rsquo;d have gotten the story right, though. I know what you mean by &rsquo;leaf in a hurricane&rsquo;; I&rsquo;ve always been able to glimpse the pieces, and I have a good grasp of the overall picture, but I was never able to figure out how it all fits together,&rdquo; I admitted.
I looked around, part of my mind trying to place myself in an unfamiliar environment, wondering whose home we were in. Where was the guy she&rsquo;d been speaking to when I woke up? What compelled a stranger to take a strange couple into his home? It was a blank that would have needed an answer if I was writing this scenario. Actually, it was a blank I&rsquo;d need to fill in somehow, now that I&rsquo;d fallen into the story I was supposed to write.
Oh, nice stumbling block there. &ldquo;How the hell am I going to write the story now?&rdquo; I found myself complaining aloud.
That made her laugh, and hearing it made me want to cry. I know, that&rsquo;s odd, but it&rsquo;s a symptom of my life. My own laughter never felt or sounded right to me. I never laughed the same way twice. Something pegs my humor meter, and some weird, random sound came out of me. Sometimes it was outright creepy.
When she laughed, it sounded right&ndash;even laced with painful understanding of my complaint. She ended with a deep breath and a sigh, saying, &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know much about writing, but I assume you just write. Put the ideas down as they come to you; when you run out of ideas, take what you&rsquo;ve got and try to put them in order. I&rsquo;m guessing you&rsquo;ve tried that, though.&rdquo;
I gave her an exasperated look. &ldquo;We are talking about your life here, right?&rdquo;
She opened her mouth, thought about it for a second, then nodded with another sigh. &ldquo;You&rsquo;re right. It couldn&rsquo;t possibly be that easy. If I hadn&rsquo;t lived it&ndash;if I&rsquo;d tried to imagine it back when my life was reasonably normal&ndash;I wouldn&rsquo;t think it was possible to even dream it,&rdquo; she confessed.
Under my breath, I muttered, &ldquo;I didn&rsquo;t think it was possible to even read it.&rdquo;
She looked at me and frowned. Oops. Guess she heard that&ndash;as she quickly confirmed, &ldquo;I read it. Apart from the fact that I quickly figured out it was impossibly like my life&ndash;not so great for my sanity right then&ndash;it was a pretty gripping story.&rdquo;
My brows furrowed. &ldquo;&lsquo;Like&rsquo; your life? I thought you said it was accurate?&rdquo; I challenged.
The look she returned was mockingly skeptical. &ldquo;Do you have any idea how weird it is to see your world through the lens of an alien language and culture?&rdquo; she retorted, arms crossed and hip cocked for emphasis.
I rolled my eyes and sighed, half exasperated, half relieved. &ldquo;I know how hard it is to capture&ndash;and by that I mean, fake&ndash;the language and culture of a world I was pretty sure existed only in my imagination. At least now I know I don&rsquo;t have to go to all that extra work.&rdquo;
She shook her head in mild disbelief and shrugged. &ldquo;From my side, it seemed like someone had gone to a lot of extra work to disguise my biography. I&rsquo;ve met a few gods who would have gone to the trouble just to mess with my head.&rdquo; She looked at me, scanned the room around us, and returned her eyes to mine. &ldquo;That could apply even to this,&rdquo; she amended, in clear reference to our situation.
&ldquo;Anyway,&rdquo; she returned to the point, &ldquo;the events and situations, the essence of my thoughts and feelings all came through. Not my story the way I might write it, but the way someone with their own perspective, looking through me, would write it. It was that, more than the books about me or you, that convinced me you were out here.&rdquo;
I tucked my head down. I couldn&rsquo;t help it, really. I got an odd feeling, thinking about that conundrum. As a writer, the sky is the limit. It&rsquo;s fun to play with the notion of gods; people who personified power, shaping reality the way a writer shapes a story. It was amusing to think you could find the lives of ordinary people on the shelves of their libraries.
To confront it as a possible reality, with only one degree of separation, ought to seem frightening&ndash;at the very least, deeply disturbing. To me, right then, it just seemed preposterous. My life didn&rsquo;t make a good story. It hardly qualified as a life. She had it characterized much better as a nightmare. It was tainted by one mundane and ugly paradox. Painful, but tame and boring compared to a life like hers.
I&rsquo;m not even sure how the idea came to me, originally. I think I&rsquo;d been sketching an outline of her life, and it sort of came up after her death&ndash;which troubled me, but given where her adventures had taken her, dying wasn&rsquo;t enough to end her adventures. I could see her coming back to life somewhere down the road, but I knew the trip to the library would send her down another path first.
I never felt comfortable about exploring that path. It hurt to imagine a scene like this, writing it out but not living it. That was the reality I expect to face; not this. In a way, I guess I believed, deep down, that the story had it&rsquo;s own reality, and I wanted to preserve that faith. How could I knowingly write myself into the story without at least that part glaring back at me as pure fiction?
I&rsquo;d lied to myself enough up to that point. I didn&rsquo;t have the stomach to do it again, even if that was what the story called for. I guess that was when I started to lose the thread of the story, trying to change it to suit a broader audience. When my demons began to work their way into the fabric of the story. With a deep sigh, I confessed, &ldquo;That&rsquo;s where I fell prey to doubt.&rdquo;
She just nodded, as if she was expecting me to say that. Well, if I was her nightmare, I suppose she would. Then she said something that proved how well she knew me. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s also pretty much where you stopped lying to yourself. Isn&rsquo;t it?&rdquo;
I crossed my arms, head still bowed&ndash;almost masking my slow, timid nod&ndash;as I confirmed, &ldquo;Yeah. That&rsquo;s where my life really started to fall apart. And, where the story started to take on a different meaning for me.&rdquo; I forced myself to look up, and say what I had to say to her face, &ldquo;I began to see my inspiration in a different way, and question all the things that brought you to mind and unlocked your story.
&ldquo;It didn&rsquo;t kill the story for me, but it expanded the paradox,&rdquo; I explained, holding myself tightly. &ldquo;I started to get a better understanding of how a time paradox works, and of course, I kept getting lost trying to follow the path, searching for the way in and out&ndash;knowing how it ends while growing less and less certain about the beginning.&rdquo;
By refusing to consider direct involvement in the story on my part, I realized that I might have imposed myself on the story in a far more disruptive way. I was still debating how to bring that concern up when she broke the silence.
&ldquo;The beginning&hellip;&rdquo; she murmured softly, thoughtfully. &ldquo;We never remember the beginning,&rdquo; she continued, pursuing a notion my words kicked loose in her skull. &ldquo;We pretend not to know why&ndash;but then that is one of our favorite tricks, isn&rsquo;t it?&rdquo; she glanced up at me, smiling at my confused silence.
With her arms crossed she began to drift about the room, examining objects while continuing this strange interlocution. &ldquo;We pretend not to know many things. We can even pretend not to know the truth. The horrible truth. But the beginning?&rdquo; she paused and met my eye in the mirror across the room from the couch. &ldquo;Well there is an explanation for that one. We never remember the beginning because there never was a beginning. That&rsquo;s one of those horrible parts of the truth we choose to miss.&rdquo;
&ldquo;What do you mean?&rdquo; I asked. Her choice of words&ndash;the horrible truth&ndash;bothered me inexplicably. My own musing forgotten for the moment, I wanted to argue with her; as a writer, I wanted to point out the fact that there are many beginnings and how easy it is to remember most of them.
The urge to argue was so strong that I felt suspicious of it. It might have been an appropriate impulse for one of the philosophic, speculative debates I used to get into with my old friends at the pub, but the honest part of me knew I was trying to keep from asking &ldquo;What horrible truth?&rdquo; aloud.
She turned to face me, and the look she gave me made me feel like I asked the wrong question. I thought she was waiting for me to rephrase my query, but, before I could think of the right question to ask, she reprimanded, &ldquo;You know what I mean.&rdquo;
For a moment I didn&rsquo;t know if she was answering the question I asked or the question I kept to myself. Or were they the same thing?
&ldquo;We never remember the beginning,&rdquo; she went on, not even sparing me a second look, &ldquo;until the very end; because there is no end either. How can there be when at either moment you&rsquo;re right in the middle of it? None of which makes sense, of course, until you risk looking at the truth. I wish I could tell you that truth. Honestly.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Why couldn&rsquo;t you?&rdquo; I prompted, hoping for something to define where this conversation was heading.
She smiled to herself. A rather frightening smile. &ldquo;And pour water into a full glass?&rdquo; She laughed. It was still a mesmerizing sound. &ldquo;The truth is already there. You already understand it on some level. It just doesn&rsquo;t fit into your perspective; you don&rsquo;t know how to explain it to your self. It&rsquo;s like being in a conversation and thinking about what you want to say when you should just be listening.&rdquo;
She wasn&rsquo;t looking at me. I doubt she was looking at anything in particular. It struck me suddenly that she was holding herself posed to listen. On sheer reflex, I tuned into the background as well, and she spoke again, &ldquo;So, telling you the truth&hellip; the trick is just getting you to listen.&rdquo;
&ldquo;What?&rdquo; I blurted, startled by the fact that she had basically done just that; tricking me into listening.
&ldquo;We never remember the beginning,&rdquo; she said quietly, precisely as she had before. I looked at her with a frown, but she carried on without regarding it. &ldquo;We pretend not to know why&ndash;but then that is one of our favorite tricks, isn&rsquo;t it? We pretend not to know many things. We can even pretend not to know the truth. The horrible truth.
&ldquo;But the truth?&rdquo; she held that in the air a moment, and looked at me pointedly. She held up her hands for emphasis.
Realizing that the question was not rhetorical, I nodded.
&ldquo;The truth is a constant. It doesn&rsquo;t need to repeat itself,&rdquo; she declared. As she went on, she measured out her words, &ldquo;I can remember the beginning.&rdquo;
Before I could ask the beginning of what she cut me off with a gesture, and resumed the pose of listening. This caused my agitation to grow. I have never considered myself an insensitive person. Indeed, there were times I felt proud of my sensitivity to the subtlest of cues.
As a child I was so sensitive that there were moments when I seemed to get caught up in the thoughts and feelings of the people around me. I don&rsquo;t know where that notion could have come from, but my best guess has been that I could read the state of a person&rsquo;s mind so well from their body language that I would experience sympathetic thoughts and feelings.
What am I driving at? Well, at that moment, her gesture struck me so suddenly she might have just as well screamed &ldquo;Listen!&rdquo; at the top of her lungs.
That shock subsided quickly, but I could not shake the feeling that she was communicating&ndash;deliberately&ndash;on many levels, verbal and non-verbal. I brought my mind into focus and met her eye.
Without words, her body resonating with the charge of an epiphany, she managed to express her awareness of my realization. My eyes widened and she nodded with the faintest smile. The clearest thought chimed in my head, Ears hear, but the mind listens&hellip; the mind listens with every sense and it becomes a sense.
Can you read my mind?
I was nodding my head before I even thought to question the origin of the thought. My mind fell still, the silence ringing with heightened awareness. I didn&rsquo;t have to question what beginning she was talking about, because suddenly it was obvious what beginning people were oblivious to. The moment we become self-aware.
She smiled broadly at me and nodded assertively. Without further distractions she resumed speaking.

